- Each node is stored in a `Box<Node<T>>`. A tree has a variable-sized recursive shape, while `Box` gives each child a fixed-size pointer and owns the child node.
- Children and the root use `Option<Box<Node<T>>>`, representing either an absent link or an owned subtree. This means the tree owns all its nodes; dropping the tree recursively drops its nodes.
- `insert` takes ownership of `T`, so values are moved into the tree rather than cloned. Equal values are ignored.
- `contains` borrows the search value, and `in_order` lends references to stored values through its callback. Neither operation needs to take ownership or clone values.
- This simple recursive tree can become deeply skewed, which may cause deep recursion during traversal or drop. A self-balancing tree is preferable when worst-case depth matters.

